items/fs-every-aleph-is-regular.md-69-
items/fs-every-aleph-is-regular.md-70-## Remarks
items/fs-every-aleph-is-regular.md-71-
items/fs-every-aleph-is-regular.md:72:**Which alephs the theorem does certify.** $\aleph_0$, in ZF; and every $\aleph_{\alpha+1}$, assuming the Axiom of Choice ([[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]]). So the false claim is not wrong everywhere — it is wrong exactly where the index is a limit ordinal reached from below by a short family, and $\omega$ is the smallest such index.
items/fs-every-aleph-is-regular.md-73-
items/fs-every-aleph-is-regular.md-74-**Singularity here is about the index, not about the size.** The cofinal family that witnesses $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega) = \aleph_0$ is $n \mapsto \aleph_n$, indexed by $\omega$ because the *subscript* $\omega$ is a limit of an $\omega$-sequence. Nothing about how large $\aleph_\omega$ is enters the argument, and nothing is chosen, which is why clause (c) of [[thm-regularity-of-the-alephs]] is choice free while clause (b) is not.
items/fs-every-aleph-is-regular.md-75-
items/fs-every-aleph-is-regular.md:76:**The claim is not repaired by assuming choice.** Adding the Axiom of Choice certifies more alephs as regular, but it does not touch the witness above: the refutation is a theorem of ZF and remains one in ZFC.
--
