research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-13148-items/lem-derivatives-along-a-line-have-the-multinomial-expansion.md:55:Thus the formula at order $r+1$ follows. [step 1.2, L1, L2, L3, L4, algebra]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-13149-items/thm-finitely-many-discontinuities-integrable.md:75:[L11] Ordered-field arithmetic and the absolute value: adding a constant and multiplying by a positive quantity preserve an inequality; the order is total and transitive; $x,y \in [c,d]$ gives $|x-y| \le d-c$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]], [[lem-of-add-order]], [[lem-of-sign-rules]], [[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-interval]]). These order-arithmetic facts are stated by their sources for the strict order only; the nonstrict forms used below follow by adjoining the equality case, in which the two sides coincide.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-13150-items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:66:2.1 Well-defined: if $p/q = p'/q'$ with $q, q' \ge 1$, then $p q' = p' q$ in $\mathbb{Z}$, so [L2] gives $\iota(p)(q' \cdot 1_F) = \iota(p')(q \cdot 1_F)$, and multiplying by the positive $(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1}(q' \cdot 1_F)^{-1}$ yields $\iota(p)(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1} = \iota(p')(q' \cdot 1_F)^{-1}$; thus $\iota(x)$ is independent of the representative. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L2, L3]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:13151:items/ex-convex-subsets-of-rn-are-path-connected.md:80:[A6] $\mathbb{R}^n$ is a real vector space, so it is closed under the scalar multiples and sums forming $(1-t)x + ty$ ([[def-vector-space]]); and an order-convex $J \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ contains every real lying between two of its elements ([[def-interval]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-13152-items/ex-rationals-and-reals-as-fields.md:10:deps: [def-field, lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring, def-zero-divisor-and-integral-domain, def-ordered-ring, lem-ordered-ring-cone-and-order-agree, lem-ordered-field-is-an-ordered-ring, def-ordered-field, def-ring-characteristic, lem-integer-multiples-agree-with-canonical-natural, def-canonical-natural, lem-of-naturals-positive, thm-rat-field, thm-reals-field, thm-rat-ordered-field, thm-reals-ordered-field, def-ring, def-commutative-ring]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-13153-items/cex-spikes-with-integral-one-converging-pointwise-to-zero.md:73:[L8] Ordered-field arithmetic and linearity of the integral: multiplying inequalities by positive reals, transitivity, and $\int_p^q(\lambda u) = \lambda\int_p^q u$ ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]], [[def-interval]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-13154-items/lem-integer-part.md:62:[L1] The embeddings $\mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{R}$ are injective and preserve $0$, $1$, addition, multiplication and order ([[lem-nat-embeds-int]], [[lem-int-embeds-rat]], [[lem-rat-embeds-dense]], [[def-integers]]); $\mathbb{Z}$ is a totally ordered commutative ring ([[thm-int-ordered-ring]], [[thm-int-comm-ring]]); every integer $\ge 0$ is the image of a unique natural, that map being injective and order preserving ([[lem-nat-embeds-int]]); and a natural $j \ne 0$ satisfies $j \ge 1$ ([[lem-nat-discrete]], [[def-natural-numbers]]).
--
